### When should you use LinkedHashMap over HashMap?

LinkedHashMap is the right choice when the order of entries matters. Here are the specific scenarios:

#### Scenario 1: Preserving insertion order for serialization

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class JsonOrderDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // HashMap: field order is unpredictable in JSON output
        Map<String, Object> hashResponse = new HashMap<>();
        hashResponse.put("status", "SUCCESS");
        hashResponse.put("accountId", "SAV2001");
        hashResponse.put("balance", 150000);
        System.out.println("HashMap: " + hashResponse);

        // LinkedHashMap: fields appear in insertion order
        Map<String, Object> linkedResponse = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        linkedResponse.put("status", "SUCCESS");
        linkedResponse.put("accountId", "SAV2001");
        linkedResponse.put("balance", 150000);
        System.out.println("LinkedHashMap: " + linkedResponse);
    }
}
```

```text
HashMap: {balance=150000, accountId=SAV2001, status=SUCCESS}
LinkedHashMap: {status=SUCCESS, accountId=SAV2001, balance=150000}
```

#### Scenario 2: Building an LRU cache

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class BalanceCacheService {
    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> cache;

    public BalanceCacheService(int maxSize) {
        this.cache = new LinkedHashMap<>(maxSize * 4 / 3 + 1, 0.75f, true) {
            @Override
            protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<String, BigDecimal> eldest) {
                boolean shouldEvict = size() > maxSize;
                if (shouldEvict) {
                    System.out.println("Evicting: " + eldest.getKey());
                }
                return shouldEvict;
            }
        };
    }

    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        return cache.get(accountId); // moves to end in access-order mode
    }

    public void updateBalance(String accountId, BigDecimal balance) {
        cache.put(accountId, balance);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BalanceCacheService service = new BalanceCacheService(3);
        service.updateBalance("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("150000"));
        service.updateBalance("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("75000"));
        service.updateBalance("FD4001", new BigDecimal("500000"));

        // Access SAV2001 – moves to end (most recently used)
        service.getBalance("SAV2001");

        // Add new entry – evicts CUR3001 (least recently used)
        service.updateBalance("LON5001", new BigDecimal("200000"));
    }
}
```

```text
Evicting: CUR3001
```

#### Scenario 3: Deterministic testing

```java
// HashMap: iteration order may differ between JVM runs
// This makes test assertions fragile
Map<String, String> hashMap = new HashMap<>();
hashMap.put("A", "1"); hashMap.put("B", "2");
// hashMap.toString() might be "{A=1, B=2}" or "{B=2, A=1}"

// LinkedHashMap: always the same order
Map<String, String> linkedMap = new LinkedHashMap<>();
linkedMap.put("A", "1"); linkedMap.put("B", "2");
// linkedMap.toString() is always "{A=1, B=2}"
```

### How does access-order mode work internally?

In access-order mode, every `get()` or `put()` on an existing key moves the entry to the tail of the doubly-linked list. The head of the list is the least recently used entry. This is the foundation of LRU behavior.

Internally, each entry has `before` and `after` pointers. On access, the entry is unlinked from its current position and re-linked at the tail. This is O(1) because it only updates 4-6 pointers.

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Iterating a LinkedHashMap in access-order mode causes ConcurrentModificationException

```java
Map<String, String> map = new LinkedHashMap<>(16, 0.75f, true);
map.put("A", "1"); map.put("B", "2"); map.put("C", "3");

// WRONG: get() during iteration modifies the linked list (structural change)
try {
    for (var entry : map.entrySet()) {
        map.get(entry.getKey()); // moves entry → ConcurrentModificationException!
    }
} catch (ConcurrentModificationException e) {
    System.out.println("CME: get() in access-order mode is structural");
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Access-order get() is a structural modification.** In access-order mode, `get()` moves the entry in the linked list. This counts as a structural modification, so `get()` during iteration throws CME. In insertion-order mode, `get()` does not modify the list.

- **LinkedHashMap is not thread-safe.** For a concurrent LRU cache, use `ConcurrentHashMap` with manual eviction or a library like Caffeine or Guava's `CacheBuilder`.

- **removeEldestEntry is called after every put.** If it returns true, the eldest entry (head of linked list) is removed. The method receives the eldest entry as a parameter so you can log or process it before eviction.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you implement an LRU cache in Java?" – LinkedHashMap with accessOrder=true + removeEldestEntry.
- "What is the memory overhead of LinkedHashMap?" – ~8 bytes per entry for before/after pointers.
- "Is get() safe during iteration in access-order mode?" – No, it throws CME because it is a structural modification.
